  "title": "Genesis 1–11: the questions readers bring",
  "status": "Study 50 · Explore the Bible · First content draft · 2026-11-06",
  "lead": [
   "Most people open the first pages of the Bible with a question already in hand. How long were the days? Where did Cain find a wife? Did Methuselah really live 969 years? Who were the Nephilim? These are fair questions, and the text can bear them. Genesis tells the story of the whole world in eleven chapters, and then, in one verse, narrows to one man:"
  ],
  "quote": {
   "k": "quote",
   "r": "GEN.12.1",
   "label": "Genesis 12:1",
   "text": "Then the LORD said to Abram, “Leave your country, your kindred, and your father’s household, and go to the land I will show you.”",
   "part": false
  },
  "close": {
   "title": "Read Genesis 1–11 again",
   "paras": [
    [
     "You came to these chapters with questions, and they are worth asking. On some of them the text speaks plainly: God made the heavens and the earth; Adam was a real man; the flood was a real judgment; every nation comes from one man. On others it is silent, and Christians who hold the whole Bible as true read it differently. Ask the three questions again on each: what the text says and leaves unsaid, where the rest of Scripture takes it up, and which part is a reading. Hold the readings with an open hand, and what the text plainly says with a closed one."
    ],
    [
     "Then notice the question these chapters answer that you may not have brought. After the murder, the flood and the tower, will God give up on the world He made? Genesis 11 ends with the nations scattered. Genesis 12 begins with God calling one man, so that “all the families of the earth will be blessed through you” (",
     {
      "r": "GEN.12.3",
      "t": "12:3"
     },
     " · BSB). Paul calls that promise the gospel told beforehand: “The Scripture foresaw that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, and foretold the gospel to Abraham” (",
     {
      "r": "GAL.3.8",
      "t": "Galatians 3:8"
     },
     " · BSB)."
    ],
    [
     "Read the passage again before you answer."
    ]
   ],
   "question": [
    "Of the questions you brought to Genesis 1–11, has any one of them become a reason to keep God at a distance? What would change if you heard these chapters as His answer to whether He will give up on you?"
   ],
   "passage": "GEN.12.1-3"
  }
